---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const faith = assertion('a-hundred-and-sixty-congregations')

const faithSpec = bar(
  faith.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  faith.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Adherents as a share of population, 2020 (%)', labelValues: true },
)

const blufftonChurches = [
  ['Swiss Mennonite (three congregations)', 640],
  ['Methodist Episcopal', 180],
  ['German Reformed', 150],
  ['Church of Christ', 113],
  ['Presbyterian', 58],
  ["St. Mary's Catholic", 50],
  ['Evangelical Lutheran', 45],
  ['Mennonite Church of Bluffton', 31],
]
---

<Article slug="a-hundred-and-sixty-congregations">
  <AssertionCard assertion={faith} />
  <p>
    Forty-nine religious bodies report a congregation in Allen County and between them they
    report 58,696 adherents — 57.4 per cent of the county's 102,206 people. Two bodies hold
    nearly half of that: the Catholic Church, 14,732 adherents in eight congregations, and the
    non-denominational Christian churches, 11,530 in twenty-five. Seventeen congregations belong
    to historically Black denominations and sixteen are Anabaptist in origin.
  </p>
  <ChartFigure
    id="county-adherents"
    title="Allen County and its neighbours: adherents as a share of population, 2020"
    caption="Adherents are what each religious body reported, and the bodies do not share a definition — some count baptized infants, some confessing adults. The remaining share of any county is people no body reported, which is not the same as people who belong to none."
    columns={['County', 'Per cent']}
    rows={faith.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="county-adherents" set:html={JSON.stringify(faithSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The gradient across this corner of Ohio is steep and Allen sits in the middle of it. Mercer,
    Putnam and Shelby, in the German Catholic settlement country to the south and west, run 64 to
    78 per cent. Hardin, Van Wert, Logan and Hancock, to the north and east, run 31 to 41.
  </p>
  <p>
    A century earlier the county counted itself differently. A religious survey reported in 1921
    put church attendance at "about 25 per cent of the population" and communicants at "something
    like 17,000" — which, against a county of 68,223 in 1920, works out to 24.9 per cent, so the
    estimate at least checks against the census. It is not the same measurement as the 2020 one.
    One counts people who attend; the other counts people a religious body reports. This site
    holds both and draws no line between them.
  </p>
  <DataTable
    title="Bluffton in 1906, member by member"
    caption="The 1906 county history called Bluffton 'a village of churches' and printed every membership. The Swiss Mennonite figure covers St. John's, Ebenezer and the Bluffton congregation together and is not a village number; the seven village congregations come to 627, against a village of 1,783 in 1900."
    columns={['Congregation', 'Members']}
    rows={blufftonChurches}
  />
  <p>
    The oldest congregation in the county seat is disputed between two books. Trinity Methodist's
    cornerstone reads a mission in 1829 and a church organization in 1832; the Market Street
    Presbyterian church was organized in August 1833, on the first of the month with nine people
    by one account and on the twenty-fourth with fourteen charter members by the other. What all
    the accounts agree on is that Lima had organized religion before it had a town government,
    which it did not get until 1842. Its synagogue, Temple Beth Israel, was built in 1914 — the
    Jewish year 5674 — with "Mine House of Prayer for All Peoples" over the door.
  </p>
</Article>
